{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1935 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "5b3790bd350d6180063fd6788f60b240625251db7d9faa55a1e823d0e0b99976" :edb/db-before-t 1934 :edb/db-after-t 1935 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535247 50 #inst "2026-10-08T20:24:45.243Z" 13194139535247 true] [17592186087513 1100 "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x) = \\dfrac{4x^3-x}{3x^3+24}$." 13194139535247 false] [17592186087513 1100 "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x) = \\dfrac{4x^3-x}{3x^3+24}.$" 13194139535247 true] [17592186087513 1145 "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to\\infty$.\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is $x^3$. So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by $x^3$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= \\frac{4x^{3} - x}{3x^{3} + 24} \\\\\n&= ((\\frac{4x^{3}}{x^{3}} - \\frac{x}{x^{3}}))/((\\frac{3x^{3}}{x^{3}} + \\frac{24}{x^{3}})) \\\\\n&= ((4 - \\frac{1}{x^{2}}))/((3 + \\frac{24}{x^{3}}))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{x^2}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{24}{x^3}\n$$\n\nbecome very small. Therefore, as $x\\to\\infty$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= ((4 - \\frac{1}{x^{2}}))/((3 + \\frac{24}{x^{3}})) \\\\\n&= → \\frac{4 - 0}{3 + 0} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{4}{3}\n$$" 13194139535247 false] [17592186087513 1145 "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to\\infty.$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is $x^3.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by $x^3,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{4{x}^{3}-x}{3{x}^{3}+24} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{x}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{3{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{24}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(4-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(3+\\frac{24}{{x}^{3}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$ and $\\dfrac{24}{x^3}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to\\infty,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(4-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(3+\\frac{24}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{4-0}{3+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y = \\dfrac{4}{3}.$" 13194139535247 true] [17592186087514 1145 "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to\\infty$.\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is $x^4$. So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by $x^4$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= \\frac{3x^{2} + 2x}{6x^{4} - 1} \\\\\n&= ((\\frac{3x^{2}}{x^{4}} + \\frac{2x}{x^{4}}))/((\\frac{6x^{4}}{x^{4}} - \\frac{1}{x^{4}})) \\\\\n&= ((\\frac{3}{x^{2}} + \\frac{2}{x^{3}}))/((6 - \\frac{1}{x^{4}}))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{x^2}, \\dfrac{2}{x^3}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{x^4}\n$$\n\nbecome very small. Therefore, as $x\\to\\infty$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= ((\\frac{3}{x^{2}} + \\frac{2}{x^{3}}))/((6 - \\frac{1}{x^{4}})) \\\\\n&= → \\frac{0 + 0}{6 - 0} \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y = 0$." 13194139535247 false] [17592186087514 1145 "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to\\infty.$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is $x^4.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by $x^4,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}+2x}{6{x}^{4}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{2x}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{6{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})}{(6-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms  $\\dfrac{3}{x^2}, \\dfrac{2}{x^3},$ and $\\dfrac{1}{x^4}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to\\infty,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})}{(6-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{0+0}{6-0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y = 0.$" 13194139535247 true] [17592186087515 1100 "Determine the horizontal asymptote of $f(x) = \\dfrac{2x^3}{x^2-1}$." 13194139535247 false] [17592186087515 1100 "Determine the horizontal asymptote of $f(x) = \\dfrac{2x^3}{x^2-1}.$" 13194139535247 true] [17592186087515 1145 "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to\\infty$.\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is $x^2$. So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by $x^2$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= \\frac{2x^{3}}{x^{2} - 1} \\\\\n&= ((\\frac{2x^{3}}{x^{2}}))/((\\frac{x^{2}}{x^{2}} - \\frac{1}{x^{2}})) \\\\\n&= (2x)/((1 - \\frac{1}{x^{2}}))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the term\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{x^2}\n$$\n\nbecomes very small. Therefore, as $x\\to\\infty$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= (2x)/((1 - \\frac{1}{x^{2}})) \\\\\n&\\approx \\frac{2x}{1 - 0} \\\\\n&= 2x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not a constant, which means that $y = f(x)$ does not have a horizontal asymptote." 13194139535247 false] [17592186087515 1145 "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to\\infty.$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is $x^2.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by $x^2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{(1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the term $\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$ becomes very small. Therefore, as $x\\to\\infty,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2x}{(1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{2x}{1-0} \\\\  & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not a constant, which means that $y = f(x)$ does not have a horizontal asymptote." 13194139535247 true] [17592186087790 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339730 13194139535247 true] [17592186087790 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339734 13194139535247 true] [17592186087790 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339738 13194139535247 true] [17592186087790 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339762 13194139535247 true] [17592186087791 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339726 13194139535247 true] [17592186087791 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339748 13194139535247 true] [17592186087791 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339755 13194139535247 true] [17592186087791 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339769 13194139535247 true] [17592186087792 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339719 13194139535247 true] [17592186087792 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339741 13194139535247 true] [17592186087792 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339776 13194139535247 true] [17592186087792 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339783 13194139535247 true] [17592186088130 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535247 true] [17592186088130 1100 "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x) = (2x(x^{3} + 1))/(3x^{4} - 7)$." 13194139535247 false] [17592186088130 1100 "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{2x({x}^{3}+1)}{3{x}^{4}-7}.$" 13194139535247 true] [17592186088130 1145 "The given function can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{4}+2x}{3{x}^{4}-7}\n$$\n\nTo find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{4}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{4},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2{x}^{4}+2x}{3{x}^{4}-7} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{2{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{2x}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{7}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})}{(3-\\frac{7}{{x}^{4}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}$ and $\\frac{7}{{x}^{4}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(2+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})}{(3-\\frac{7}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{2+0}{3-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=\\frac{2}{3}.$" 13194139535247 true] [17592186088221 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535247 true] [17592186088221 1100 "Determine the horizontal asymptote of $f(x) = \\frac{x^{3} + 3}{4x^{2} - 6x}$." 13194139535247 false] [17592186088221 1100 "Determine the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{3}+3}{4{x}^{2}-6x}.$" 13194139535247 true] [17592186088221 1145 "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{2}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{3}+3}{4{x}^{2}-6x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{6x}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}})}{(4-\\frac{6}{x})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}$ and $\\frac{6}{x}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(x+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}})}{(4-\\frac{6}{x})} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{x+0}{4-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not a constant, which means that $y=f(x)$ does not have a horizontal asymptote." 13194139535247 true] [17592186088224 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535247 true] [17592186088224 1100 "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x) = \\frac{2x + 1}{9x + 8x^{2} - x^{3}}$." 13194139535247 false] [17592186088224 1100 "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{2x+1}{9x+8{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}}.$" 13194139535247 true] [17592186088224 1145 "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{3}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2x+1}{9x+8{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{2x}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{9x}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{8{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{9}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{8}{x}-1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}},\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}},\\frac{9}{{x}^{2}}$ and $\\frac{8}{x}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{9}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{8}{x}-1)} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{0+0}{0+0-1} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=0.$" 13194139535247 true] [17592186339719 1017 #edb/ref 17592186339720 13194139535247 true] [17592186339719 1089 "q-113178" 13194139535247 true] [17592186339719 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535247 true] [17592186339719 1100 "Determine the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{4}-3x+2}{{x}^{2}+1}.$" 13194139535247 true] [17592186339719 1113 #uuid "98127c5e-809c-59c2-bd79-ffd78a0e8150" 13194139535247 true] [17592186339719 1145 "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{2}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{4}-3x+2}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{3x}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}-\\frac{3}{x}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})}{(1+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{3}{x},$ $\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}$ and $\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{({x}^{2}-\\frac{3}{x}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})}{(1+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{{x}^{2}-0+0}{1+0} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not a constant, which means that $y=f(x)$ does not have a horizontal asymptote." 13194139535247 true] [17592186339720 1000 #edb/ref 17592186339721 13194139535247 true] [17592186339720 1000 #edb/ref 17592186339722 13194139535247 true] [17592186339720 1000 #edb/ref 17592186339723 13194139535247 true] [17592186339720 1000 #edb/ref 17592186339724 13194139535247 true] [17592186339720 1000 #edb/ref 17592186339725 13194139535247 true] [17592186339720 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535247 true] [17592186339720 1041 "selection" 13194139535247 true] [17592186339720 1104 #edb/ref 17592186339721 13194139535247 true] [17592186339720 1110 #uuid "026eb333-7fe3-509a-a7be-815358835cf0" 13194139535247 true] [17592186339721 1034 "No horizontal asymptotes" 13194139535247 true] [17592186339721 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535247 true] [17592186339721 1111 #uuid "2e6d2b9b-4fc5-55f5-b0b8-4d24a4f93df6" 13194139535247 true] [17592186339722 1034 "y=-3" 13194139535247 true] [17592186339722 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535247 true] [17592186339722 1111 #uuid "d58c7b64-8918-5e5e-aec6-48bf6fa9c2b3" 13194139535247 true] [17592186339723 1034 "y=0" 13194139535247 true] [17592186339723 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535247 true] [17592186339723 1111 #uuid "1bfb1810-7ea8-57b7-9b69-530c586fb05f" 13194139535247 true] [17592186339724 1034 "y=2" 13194139535247 true] [17592186339724 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535247 true] [17592186339724 1111 #uuid "98ec2de6-efe2-5639-97e3-70eed9db85f7" 13194139535247 true] [17592186339725 1034 "y=1" 13194139535247 true] [17592186339725 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535247 true] [17592186339725 1111 #uuid "cb1d8512-c4a3-5f5f-a68b-5671b307a8ec" 13194139535247 true] [17592186339726 1017 #edb/ref 17592186339727 13194139535247 true] [17592186339726 1089 "q-269988" 13194139535247 true] [17592186339726 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535247 true] [17592186339726 1100 "The horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{7{x}^{3}+2x-5}{{x}^{2}(2{x}^{2}+3)}$ is $y=${{field-1}}." 13194139535247 true] [17592186339726 1113 #uuid "beaf7cea-b83a-5727-986d-b09c501e0dda" 13194139535247 true] [17592186339726 1145 "The given function can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{7{x}^{3}+2x-5}{2{x}^{4}+3{x}^{2}}\n$$\n\nTo find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{4}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{4},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{7{x}^{3}+2x-5}{2{x}^{4}+3{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{7{x}^{3}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{2x}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{5}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{2{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{7}{x}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{5}{{x}^{4}})}{(2+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{7}{x},$ $\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}},$ $\\frac{5}{{x}^{4}},$ and $\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{7}{x}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{5}{{x}^{4}})}{(2+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{0+0-0}{2+0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=0.$" 13194139535247 true] [17592186339727 1000 #edb/ref 17592186339728 13194139535247 true] [17592186339727 1000 #edb/ref 17592186339729 13194139535247 true] [17592186339727 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535247 true] [17592186339727 1041 "field-1" 13194139535247 true] [17592186339727 1104 #edb/ref 17592186339729 13194139535247 true] [17592186339727 1110 #uuid "7cee78a3-8515-5442-ab24-23c06e0410b1" 13194139535247 true] [17592186339728 1034 "1" 13194139535247 true] [17592186339728 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535247 true] [17592186339728 1111 #uuid "65e86732-e23d-595c-bbfc-1848f151b0df" 13194139535247 true] [17592186339729 1034 "0" 13194139535247 true] [17592186339729 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535247 true] [17592186339729 1111 #uuid "c8d1141b-25e5-56ea-b417-0514bd6b08d7" 13194139535247 true] [17592186339730 1017 #edb/ref 17592186339731 13194139535247 true] [17592186339730 1089 "q-277944" 13194139535247 true] [17592186339730 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535247 true] [17592186339730 1100 "The horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-8{x}^{3}}{2{x}^{3}-5x}$ is $y=${{field-1}}." 13194139535247 true] [17592186339730 1113 #uuid "ce3539b4-30f1-5ce1-a505-47ade32dd7c9" 13194139535247 true] [17592186339730 1145 "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn this case, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{3}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-8{x}^{3}}{2{x}^{3}-5x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{8{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{5x}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x}-8)}{(2-\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{1}{x}$ and $\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x}-8)}{(2-\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{0-8}{2+0} \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-4.$" 13194139535247 true] [17592186339731 1000 #edb/ref 17592186339732 13194139535247 true] [17592186339731 1000 #edb/ref 17592186339733 13194139535247 true] [17592186339731 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535247 true] [17592186339731 1041 "field-1" 13194139535247 true] [17592186339731 1104 #edb/ref 17592186339732 13194139535247 true] [17592186339731 1110 #uuid "c945c694-1169-5142-8b42-5f4a3989b151" 13194139535247 true] [17592186339732 1034 "-4" 13194139535247 true] [17592186339732 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535247 true] [17592186339732 1111 #uuid "e50a09ba-5999-52cc-9af4-c94ee64d2e85" 13194139535247 true] [17592186339733 1034 "4" 13194139535247 true] [17592186339733 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535247 true] [17592186339733 1111 #uuid "24af47c0-2640-5c41-b998-cd09c77a329f" 13194139535247 true] [17592186339734 1017 #edb/ref 17592186339735 13194139535247 true] [17592186339734 1089 "q-277946" 13194139535247 true] [17592186339734 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535247 true] [17592186339734 1100 "The horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{4{x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}+3}{2-{x}^{3}}$ is $y=${{field-1}}." 13194139535247 true] [17592186339734 1113 #uuid "9bd55036-b9b9-5eb4-a252-ea929e1a8c76" 13194139535247 true] [17592186339734 1145 "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, 